00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:29.260
B.C. policy solutions put that hourly figure in its 2026 living wage report,

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.460
the pay-of-full-time worker needs for essentials, to avoid chronic money stress,

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.600
and still take part in community life.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.820
Daily Hive and Business and Vancouver both carry the release.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:44.420
The new Metro number is a 2.5% climb from 2025's $27.85,

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.480
and it sits $10.30 over B.C.'s $18.25 minimum.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.600
The institute says wages still are not keeping up,

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.740
and it links pricier food, fuel, clothing and footwear to the U.S. trade war and the war

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:58.120
in Iran.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.460
35% of workers here, more than half a million people, earn under that living wage.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:08.660
Lead author Eglika Ivanova, the institute's co-executive director and senior economist,

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:14.080
says Canada Mortgage and Housing Corporation data put rent cuts and empty units in new high-end

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:19.360
buildings while lower income households, especially families who need two bedrooms or more, still

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:20.340
face a tight market.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.760
Food is the second biggest line in that budget and rose 4.1 percent in a year.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.660
Out of pocket child care for the living wage family of four, rose by nearly $160 a month.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:35.120
The report says the wage would have been higher still without the Canadian dental care plan,

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:39.560
the Canada groceries and essentials benefit, and the BC rental assistance program,

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:44.860
and that scrapping the BC climate action tax credit hit low wage earners who do not have children.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:50.460
Co-author Navdeep Singh Chinna, Managing Director of Living Wage BC, asked the incoming

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.600
provincial government to spend on affordable housing, public transit, and childcare.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:04.220
Outside the region, Squamish is at $29.5, up 3.8% and Whistler is at $30.50, up 3%.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.580
Grand Forks is the provincial low at $22.15.

